---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { Content as Agents } from '../ai/AGENTS.md';
import { Content as DesignDoc } from '../ai/examples/design_trafficwhatif_engine.md';
import protocolSrc from '../ai/examples/theta_train.h?raw';
import codeSrc from '../ai/examples/build_loss_mask.py?raw';

const tiers = [
  {
    k: 'Design',
    file: 'doc/design/*.md',
    owner: 'Human only',
    ai: 'Read-only. AI may never edit, rename, move or delete these files.',
    what: 'The research problem, the overall architecture, and the decisions that make the project what it is.',
    cls: 'human',
  },
  {
    k: 'Protocol',
    file: 'src/protocol/*.h',
    owner: 'Human + AI',
    ai: 'Drafts with me. Edits an existing protocol only with my explicit permission. Must not contradict the design.',
    what: 'One interface per component: purpose, config and output contracts, functions, return structures, and a step-by-step workflow. Detailed enough to understand the program without reading the code.',
    cls: 'shared',
  },
  {
    k: 'Implementation',
    file: 'src/*.py',
    owner: 'AI-led',
    ai: 'Writes the code, strictly under AGENTS.md: modular, functional, documented, fail-fast.',
    what: 'The repetitive part. The code must match its protocol step by step, so review becomes comparing code against the protocol.',
    cls: 'ai',
  },
];
---
<Base title="AI Usage" description="How Dexin Ren uses AI coding agents responsibly: humans own design and key decisions, AI does the repetitive work under explicit rules.">
  <p class="eyebrow">AI usage</p>
  <h1>How I use AI</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    AI agents write much of my code. They do not design my research. The rule is simple: <strong>AI does the repetitive work,
    and my time goes to the problem, the design and the hard decisions.</strong> To keep that true, I use a fixed chain of authority
    that the agent cannot rewrite.
  </p>

  <h2>Chain of authority</h2>
  <ol class="tiers" aria-label="Design authority from highest to lowest">
    {tiers.map((t, i) => (
      <li class:list={['tier', t.cls]}>
        <div class="tier-head">
          <span class="lvl">{i + 1}</span>
          <div>
            <p class="tk">{t.k}</p>
            <code class="tf">{t.file}</code>
          </div>
          <span class="owner">{t.owner}</span>
        </div>
        <p class="what">{t.what}</p>
        <p class="perm"><strong>AI:</strong> {t.ai}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>
  <p class="small muted">Higher levels override lower ones. A protocol may not contradict the design, and code may not contradict its protocol.</p>

  <h2>When a request conflicts with the design</h2>
  <p>The agent may not quietly “solve” a conflict by redesigning through code. It has to stop and hand the decision back to me:</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Stop</strong> before editing any implementation.</li>
    <li><strong>Explain</strong> the conflict and which design or protocol rule it breaks.</li>
    <li><strong>Propose</strong> the protocol change that would be needed.</li>
    <li><strong>Wait</strong> for explicit approval. If the <em>design</em> itself must change, I edit the design document myself first.</li>
    <li><strong>Then</strong> update the protocol, then the code to match.</li>
  </ol>

  <aside class="anchor" aria-labelledby="anchor-title">
    <p class="anchor-k">Technique</p>
    <h3 id="anchor-title">Context anchor</h3>
    <p class="anchor-line"><span>“I should read AGENTS.md next and treat it as my first priority.”</span></p>
    <p>
      In a long session, the instructions an agent read at the start slowly drop out of its working context. The freshest thing it
      sees is its own last reply. So every reply ends with this sentence, which points straight back to the rules. The agent re-reads
      AGENTS.md before the next task, and always after its context has been summarized or compacted.
    </p>
    <p class="anchor-note">It reduces forgetting rather than preventing it. The anchor only works together with the re-read rules in AGENTS.md.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>Why it works this way</h2>
  <ul class="why">
    <li><strong>The human holds the intellectual core.</strong> The research question, the design and the trade-offs are written by me, in files the agent cannot touch.</li>
    <li><strong>Review is tractable.</strong> I review every protocol before it goes into production. Because code must follow its protocol’s numbered workflow, checking an implementation means comparing two documents, not reverse-engineering a large diff.</li>
    <li><strong>Errors stay visible.</strong> The rules forbid broad <code>try/except</code>, silent defaults and guessed data. When an assumption breaks, the program stops loudly instead of producing a plausible but wrong result. For research code this matters more than convenience.</li>
    <li><strong>The rules don’t fade.</strong> Each reply ends with a <a href="#anchor-title">context anchor</a> that sends the agent back to its rules.</li>
    <li><strong>The code stays functional.</strong> Small pure functions with explicit inputs and outputs, and side effects kept at the edges. That makes each piece easy to test and review, and a change in one place cannot quietly ripple into another.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>One real example from each layer</h2>
  <p>
    The three files below come from my own projects. They show what each layer looks like in practice. The protocol and the
    code are a matched pair: the protocol entry for <code>build_loss_mask</code> is the contract, and the function below it is
    the implementation that has to honour it.
  </p>
  <div class="ex" data-ex>
    <div class="ex-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Example layer">
      <button type="button" role="tab" class="ex-tab" aria-selected="true" data-k="0"><span class="ex-n">1</span> Design</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" class="ex-tab" aria-selected="false" data-k="1"><span class="ex-n">2</span> Protocol</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" class="ex-tab" aria-selected="false" data-k="2"><span class="ex-n">3</span> Code</button>
    </div>

    <section class="ex-panel" role="tabpanel" data-p="0">
      <p class="ex-meta">
        <strong>TrafficWhatIf · <code>doc/design/Overview-Engine.md</code></strong><br />
        Written by me alone. It fixes the goal, the components, how they connect, and the one principle everything else must respect.
        Agents can read it but cannot change it. TrafficWhatIf is still in development, so the detailed design is removed here.
      </p>
      <div class="ex-box prose"><DesignDoc /></div>
    </section>

    <section class="ex-panel" role="tabpanel" data-p="1" hidden>
      <p class="ex-meta">
        <strong>RectifiedTraj · <code>src/protocol/theta_train.h</code></strong><br />
        The interface for the training script: shared-state rules, every function's purpose, inputs, outputs and behaviour, and
        the overall flow, with no implementation. Written together with the agent and reviewed by me before any code was written
        against it. Look for <code>build_loss_mask</code>. RectifiedTraj used an earlier version of this workflow, so the format is
        slightly simpler than the one AGENTS.md now prescribes.
      </p>
      <pre class="ex-box code"><code>{protocolSrc}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="ex-panel" role="tabpanel" data-p="2" hidden>
      <p class="ex-meta">
        <strong>RectifiedTraj · <code>src/theta_train.py</code> (excerpt)</strong><br />
        The implementation of <code>build_loss_mask</code>. The policy names, the head/body regions and the return value match the
        protocol entry. Invalid input fails immediately with a clear error instead of falling back to a default.
      </p>
      <pre class="ex-box code"><code>{codeSrc}</code></pre>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2>The AGENTS.md I use</h2>
  <p>
    These are the rules every coding agent in my projects reads first: a context-refresh rule, design principles, design authority, the protocol format,
    a functional programming style, documentation, return and error-handling policy, review priorities, and a definition of done.
    This is the general version I start each project from; a project can add stricter rules where its design needs them.
    Feel free to reuse or adapt it.
  </p>
  <div class="agents-actions">
    <a class="btn" href="/AGENTS.md" download="AGENTS.md"><Icon name="download" />Download AGENTS.md</a>
    <button class="btn ghost" type="button" id="copy-agents"><Icon name="file" />Copy</button>
  </div>
  <details class="agents" id="agents">
    <summary>Read the full AGENTS.md <span class="muted small">(about 300 lines)</span></summary>
    <div class="agents-body prose"><Agents /></div>
  </details>

  <p class="small muted note">
    The examples are shared deliberately. The TrafficWhatIf design overview is shown with its detailed design removed; its full
    design documents, protocols and code stay private until the work is published. RectifiedTraj is published, and its code is MIT-licensed. This website was also
    built with an AI assistant: I decided the structure, the content and what could be disclosed, and reviewed the result.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .tiers { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 1.5rem 0 .75rem; display: grid; gap: 0; }
  .tier { position: relative; margin: 0; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 18px 20px; }
  .tier + .tier { margin-top: 28px; }
  .tier + .tier::before { content: '↓'; position: absolute; top: -24px; left: 32px; color: var(--ink-3); font-size: .95rem; }
  .tier.human { border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); }
  .tier.shared { border-left: 4px solid var(--series-4); }
  .tier.ai { border-left: 4px solid var(--series-2); }
  .tier-head { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px; }
  .lvl { flex: none; display: grid; place-items: center; width: 30px; height: 30px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--surface-2); color: var(--ink-2); font-weight: 600; font-size: .85rem; }
  .tk { margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.25rem; line-height: 1.2; }
  .tf { font-size: .78rem; }
  .owner { margin-left: auto; font-size: .75rem; font-weight: 600; padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--surface-2); color: var(--ink-2); white-space: nowrap; }
  .human .owner { background: var(--accent-soft); color: var(--accent-ink); }
  .what { margin: 12px 0 6px; color: var(--ink-2); font-size: .95rem; }
  .perm { margin: 0; font-size: .88rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .perm strong { color: var(--ink-2); }

  .steps { padding-left: 1.3em; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .steps strong, .why strong { color: var(--ink); }
  .why { color: var(--ink-2); }
  .why li { margin: .55rem 0; }

  .ex { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 14px; margin: 1.25rem 0 2rem; }
  .ex-tabs { display: flex; gap: 6px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-bottom: 12px; }
  .ex-tab { font: inherit; font-size: .85rem; display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 7px; padding: 6px 13px 6px 7px; border-radius: 999px; border: 1px solid var(--line-strong); background: transparent; color: var(--ink-2); cursor: pointer; }
  .ex-n { display: inline-grid; place-items: center; width: 20px; height: 20px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--surface-2); font-size: .72rem; font-weight: 600; }
  .ex-tab[aria-selected='true'] { background: var(--accent); border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--on-accent); }
  .ex-tab[aria-selected='true'] .ex-n { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--on-accent) 22%, transparent); }
  .ex-meta { font-size: .88rem; color: var(--ink-2); margin: 0 4px 12px; }
  .ex-meta strong { color: var(--ink); }
  .ex-box { max-height: 62vh; overflow: auto; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); background: var(--bg); margin: 0; }
  .ex-box.prose { padding: 4px 20px 16px; font-size: .92rem; }
  .ex-box.prose :global(h1) { font-size: 1.45rem; margin-top: 1.2rem; }
  .ex-box.prose :global(h2) { font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 1.6rem; }
  .ex-box.prose :global(pre) { background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: var(--radius-sm); overflow-x: auto; font-size: .76rem; line-height: 1.45; }
  .ex-box.prose :global(pre code) { background: none; padding: 0; }
  .ex-box.prose :global(blockquote) { margin: 1rem 0; padding: .2rem 1rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); color: var(--ink); }
  .ex-box.prose :global(hr) { margin: 1.5rem 0; }
  .ex-box.prose :global(.redacted) {
    position: relative; margin: 1rem 0; padding: 30px 16px 14px; border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    border: 1px dashed var(--line-strong); color: var(--ink-3); font-size: .85rem;
    background: repeating-linear-gradient(-45deg, var(--surface-2) 0 10px, transparent 10px 20px);
  }
  .ex-box.prose :global(.redacted-tag) {
    position: absolute; top: 8px; left: 12px; font-size: .68rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--on-ctl); background: var(--ctl); padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 999px;
  }
  .ex-box.code { padding: 14px 16px; font: .78rem/1.55 var(--font-mono); color: var(--ink-2); white-space: pre; tab-size: 4; }
  .ex-box.code code { background: none; padding: 0; font-size: inherit; }

  .anchor { margin: 2rem 0 .5rem; padding: 18px 22px 14px; border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--ctl-soft); border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ctl) 35%, transparent); border-left: 4px solid var(--ctl); }
  .anchor-k { margin: 0; font-size: .7rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ctl-ink); }
  .anchor h3 { margin: .15rem 0 .8rem; font-size: 1.35rem; }
  .anchor-line { margin: 0 0 .9rem; }
  .anchor-line span { display: block; font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.45; color: var(--ink); background: var(--surface); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); padding: 12px 16px; border: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .anchor p { color: var(--ink-2); font-size: .95rem; }
  .anchor-note { font-size: .82rem !important; color: var(--ink-3) !important; margin: 0; }
  .agents-actions { display: flex; gap: 8px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin: 1rem 0; }
  .agents-actions button.btn { font: inherit; font-size: .85rem; cursor: pointer; }
  .agents { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); }
  .agents summary { cursor: pointer; padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 600; font-size: .95rem; }
  .agents-body { padding: 4px 22px 18px; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); max-height: 70vh; overflow-y: auto; font-size: .92rem; }
  .agents-body :global(h1) { font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: 1.2rem; }
  .agents-body :global(h2) { font-size: 1.2rem; margin-top: 1.8rem; }
  .agents-body :global(h3) { font-size: 1.05rem; }
  .agents-body :global(pre) { background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: var(--radius-sm); overflow-x: auto; font-size: .8rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .agents-body :global(pre code) { background: none; padding: 0; }
  .agents-body :global(hr) { margin: 1.5rem 0; }
  .note { margin-top: 2.5rem; }

  @media (max-width: 639px) {
    .tier-head { flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .owner { margin-left: 44px; }
    .agents-body { padding: 4px 14px 14px; }
  }
</style>

<script>
  import raw from '../ai/AGENTS.md?raw';

  // Tabs for the three example layers
  const ex = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ex]')!;
  const exTabs = Array.from(ex.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.ex-tab'));
  const exPanels = Array.from(ex.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.ex-panel'));
  exTabs.forEach((t, k) => t.addEventListener('click', () => {
    exTabs.forEach((x, j) => x.setAttribute('aria-selected', String(j === k)));
    exPanels.forEach((p, j) => (p.hidden = j !== k));
  }));

  const b = document.getElementById('copy-agents')!;
  b.addEventListener('click', async () => {
    const label = b.lastChild!;
    try { await navigator.clipboard.writeText(raw); label.textContent = 'Copied'; }
    catch { label.textContent = 'Copy failed'; }
    setTimeout(() => (label.textContent = 'Copy'), 1500);
  });
</script>
